  "body": "## Finding description and impact\n\nIn `_validateSignature`, three error branches are handled after `ECDSA.tryRecover`:\n\n```solidity\n(address recovered, ECDSA.RecoverError recoverError, bytes32 errorArg) = ECDSA.tryRecover(hash, signature);\n\nif (recoverError == ECDSA.RecoverError.InvalidSignatureLength) {\n    revert AtomWallet_InvalidSignatureLength(uint256(errorArg));  // reverts\n} else if (recoverError == ECDSA.RecoverError.InvalidSignatureS) {\n    revert AtomWallet_InvalidSignatureS(errorArg);                // reverts\n} else if (recoverError == ECDSA.RecoverError.InvalidSignature) {\n    return _packValidationData(true, validUntil, validAfter);     // correct: returns SIG_VALIDATION_FAILED\n}\n```\n\nThe `InvalidSignature` branch correctly returns `_packValidationData(true, ...)` (i.e. `SIG_VALIDATION_FAILED`). However, the `InvalidSignatureS` branch unconditionally reverts instead.\n\n`RecoverError.InvalidSignatureS` is triggered by OpenZeppelin's ECDSA library when the `s` component of a signature falls in the upper half of the secp256k1 curve order (high-s signature). While modern wallets following EIP-2 canonical signature rules produce low-s signatures, high-s signatures are technically valid ECDSA signatures. Crucially, any observer of a valid owner signature can derive a high-s counterpart by computing `s' = curve_order - s` — the recovered address is identical, making this a constructable input against any live wallet.\n\nThis is similar to this older finding: https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-01-biconomy-findings/issues/516\n\nThe ERC-4337 (https://github.com/ethereum/ercs/blob/master/ERCS/erc-4337.md) specification is explicit:\n\n> \"MUST validate that the signature is a valid signature of the userOpHash, and SHOULD return SIG_VALIDATION_FAILED (1) without reverting on signature mismatch.\"\n\nWhen `validateUserOp` reverts instead of returning `SIG_VALIDATION_FAILED`, the ERC-4337 EntryPoint cannot distinguish a signature failure from a critical wallet bug. During on-chain `handleOps` execution, the EntryPoint calls `validateUserOp` without a try/catch — a revert propagates through the entire bundle, causing the bundler's transaction to fail.\n\nNote: the README's known issue states \"AtomWallet signature format is intentionally strict: only 65-byte raw ECDSA or 77-byte ECDSA+time-window suffix.\" This addresses signature *length*, not s-value canonicity. A 65-byte high-s signature satisfies the length requirement and is not covered by this known issue.\n\nOn top of not being compliant with the standard we also have the following impacts:\n\n1. **Adversary-constructable griefing**: Given any valid on-chain ECDSA signature from an AtomWallet owner (observable after any executed UserOp), an adversary derives the high-s counterpart: `s' = secp256k1_order - s`, `v' = v XOR 1`. The recovered address is identical to the original signer, so the signature appears legitimate until `tryRecover` rejects the high-s value. The adversary can then repeatedly submit griefing UserOps targeting any AtomWallet.\n\n2. **Bundler availability disruption**: When bundlers simulate the griefing UserOp, `validateUserOp` reverts. Depending on EntryPoint version and bundler implementation: (a) the simulation failure wastes bundler compute on every griefing submission, (b) if the bundler's error handling does not distinguish a wallet revert from a transient failure, it may retry or blacklist the wallet, and (c) in EntryPoint implementations where validation reverts propagate to the outer `handleOps` call, the bundler's entire batch transaction fails on-chain, causing gas loss.\n\n3. **Wallet degradation**: Persistent griefing can cause bundlers to deprioritize or refuse to service the targeted AtomWallet, degrading the user's ability to submit transactions through the ERC-4337 infrastructure.\n\n## Recommended mitigation steps\n\nReturn `SIG_VALIDATION_FAILED` for `InvalidSignatureS`, consistent with how `InvalidSignature` is already handled:\n\n```solidity\nif (recoverError == ECDSA.RecoverError.InvalidSignatureLength) {\n    return _packValidationData(true, validUntil, validAfter);\n} else if (recoverError == ECDSA.RecoverError.InvalidSignatureS) {\n    return _packValidationData(true, validUntil, validAfter);\n} else if (recoverError == ECDSA.RecoverError.InvalidSignature) {\n    return _packValidationData(true, validUntil, validAfter);\n}\n```\n\nOr more concisely, return `SIG_VALIDATION_FAILED` for any non-`NoError` recovery result.",
  "proof_of_concept": "```solidity\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: BUSL-1.1\npragma solidity 0.8.29;\n\nimport { console } from \"forge-std/src/Test.sol\";\nimport { TransparentUpgradeableProxy } from \"@openzeppelin/contracts/proxy/transparent/TransparentUpgradeableProxy.sol\";\nimport { PackedUserOperation } from \"@account-abstraction/interfaces/PackedUserOperation.sol\";\n\nimport { AtomWallet } from \"src/protocol/wallet/AtomWallet.sol\";\nimport { ProgressiveCurve } from \"src/protocol/curves/ProgressiveCurve.sol\";\nimport { OffsetProgressiveCurve } from \"src/protocol/curves/OffsetProgressiveCurve.sol\";\nimport { BaseTest } from \"./BaseTest.t.sol\";\n\ncontract PoCCore is BaseTest {\n    uint256 constant CURVE_ORDER = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFEBAAEDCE6AF48A03BBFD25E8CD0364141;\n\n    // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    // [L-01] AtomWallet._validateSignature reverts on InvalidSignatureS\n    // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n    /// @notice Proves that validateUserOp REVERTS when given a high-s ECDSA signature, violating\n    ///         ERC-4337 which requires returning SIG_VALIDATION_FAILED instead.\n    ///\n    ///         Attack path: any observer of a valid UserOp can derive a high-s variant by\n    ///         computing s' = secp256k1_order - s and flipping v.  The recovered address is\n    ///         identical, so the signature is \"wrong\" only by s-value canonicity — yet AtomWallet\n    ///         reverts instead of returning SIG_VALIDATION_FAILED as the spec mandates.\n    function test_L01_highS_reverts_instead_of_SIG_VALIDATION_FAILED() external {\n        // ── Setup: AtomWallet owned by a known keypair ──\n        uint256 ownerPrivKey = 0xA11CE;\n        address owner = vm.addr(ownerPrivKey);\n        address ep = makeAddr(\"entryPoint\");\n\n        vm.mockCall(\n            address(protocol.multiVault),\n            abi.encodeWithSelector(protocol.multiVault.getAtomWarden.selector),\n            abi.encode(owner)\n        );\n\n        AtomWallet impl = new AtomWallet();\n        TransparentUpgradeableProxy proxy = new TransparentUpgradeableProxy(address(impl), users.admin, \"\");\n        AtomWallet wallet = AtomWallet(payable(address(proxy)));\n        wallet.initialize(ep, address(protocol.multiVault), bytes32(uint256(1)));\n\n        // ── Sign a UserOp hash with the owner key (produces a canonical low-s signature) ──\n        bytes32 userOpHash = keccak256(\"testUserOp\");\n        bytes32 ethHash = keccak256(abi.encodePacked(\"\\x19Ethereum Signed Message:\\n32\", userOpHash));\n        (uint8 v, bytes32 r, bytes32 s) = vm.sign(ownerPrivKey, ethHash);\n\n        // Step 1 — Baseline: low-s signature returns SIG_VALIDATION_SUCCESS (0)\n        PackedUserOperation memory userOp;\n        userOp.signature = abi.encodePacked(r, s, v);\n\n        resetPrank(ep);\n        uint256 result = wallet.validateUserOp(userOp, userOpHash, 0);\n        vm.stopPrank();\n        assertEq(result, 0, \"valid low-s signature must return 0 (SIG_VALIDATION_SUCCESS)\");\n\n        // Step 2 — Derive high-s variant (publicly constructable from any valid signature)\n        //          s' = curve_order - s  keeps the same recovered address; flip v 27↔28\n        bytes32 highS = bytes32(CURVE_ORDER - uint256(s));\n        uint8 flippedV = v == 27 ? 28 : 27;\n        userOp.signature = abi.encodePacked(r, highS, flippedV);\n\n        // Step 3 — ERC-4337 spec § \"Account validation\":\n        //   \"The account MUST validate the signature … MUST NOT revert on failure.\"\n        //   Correct behaviour: return _packValidationData(true, …) = SIG_VALIDATION_FAILED\n        //   Actual behaviour:  REVERT with AtomWallet_InvalidSignatureS\n        //\n        //   Because the EntryPoint calls validateUserOp without a try/catch, this revert\n        //   propagates to the bundler's handleOps transaction, causing the entire batch to fail.\n        resetPrank(ep);\n        vm.expectRevert(abi.encodeWithSelector(AtomWallet.AtomWallet_InvalidSignatureS.selector, highS));\n        wallet.validateUserOp(userOp, userOpHash, 0);\n        vm.stopPrank();\n    }\n}\n```",
